# Line endings, decided once for everyone.
#
# Without this file the ending depended on each developer's `core.autocrlf`,
# which on Windows rewrites every file to CRLF on checkout. Prettier defaults
# to LF, so `format:check` - the first step of `pnpm verify` - rejected every
# file on a Windows working copy no matter what had been committed. 21 of the
# 25 failures it reported were that, and the check could not be made to pass
# by fixing files.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text and normalise it to LF in the
# repository; `eol=lf` makes the checkout write LF too, so the working tree
# and the stored content finally agree on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, and must never be touched: normalising these corrupts them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.otf binary
*.ttf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.keystore binary
*.hbc binary
*.traineddata binary

# Windows-only scripts that genuinely need CRLF to run.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Generated and checked for byte equality by the package manager.
pnpm-lock.yaml -diff
